\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Cross-environment evidence.}
The MATH null ($p = 0.81$) validates the prediction that removing catastrophic failure eliminates routing signal; ALFWorld identifies information asymmetry as an alternative mechanism (\S\ref{sec:cross-env}). True quantitative DAF replication requires a second catastrophic-failure environment (e.g., NetHack~\citep{nethack-2020}, Crafter~\citep{crafter-2022}).

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and correlation structure.}
The $r = 0.97$ correlation is specific to catastrophic death ($P{=}100$); defining failure as any non-positive score yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$). This correlation follows algebraically from death dominance when $P$ is constant (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}); larger models with lower death rates would reduce DAF.

\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
Death avoidance dominance (78.9\% of non-tie labels, DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$) reframes modality routing as a \emph{safety} problem, closer to a conservative default policy than to cost-quality optimization~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive, cogrouter-2026}. The 86:9 label imbalance explains why always-deterministic is a strong baseline; even an oracle with perfect per-task death risk knowledge cannot outperform it (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold-routing}).

\paragraph{Framework applicability.}
The framework applies most directly to environments with sparse terminal rewards and catastrophic failure modes. Graded failure costs would yield intermediate DAF values; stronger backbones with lower hallucination rates would reduce death-rate differentials and consequently lower DAF.
